{"id":"h244","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"The cornea of the eye is a convex mirror, and the high-light usually seen in it is a reflected image. Suppose that a circular object 20 cm in diameter is located 40 cm from the eye. If the radius of the cornea is 0.8 cm, how far is the virtual image from the cornea and what is its size?\nIs the correct answer \"4 mm inside the cornea, 2 mm in diameter\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer is yes: about 4 mm behind the corneal surface and about 2 mm in diameter.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The cornea acts as a convex mirror with R = 0.8 cm, giving focal length f = -0.4 cm.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The mirror equation with object distance 40 cm gives an image distance of about -0.396 cm, a virtual image behind the surface.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The magnification is about 0.0099 and positive, so the image is upright.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The image diameter is about 0.198 cm, roughly 2 mm.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Misreading 'inside' is a risk, but a virtual image behind a convex mirror lies within the cornea region, matching the stated answer.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Rounding of the values (3.96 mm and 1.98 mm) does not change the answer at the stated precision.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The mirror and magnification formulas applied to the given data decide the answer.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.6,"